The VP9 decoder and encoder (crates/vp9, the rivet-vp9 repository,
https://github.com/rivet-transcoder/rivet-vp9, under the same licence as the
H.264 / H.265 decoders) are original code by the same author, written from
the VP9 Bitstream & Decoding Process Specification, v0.6 and v0.7 (Google and
Argon Design), with its tables reproduced as data. They do not include or
derive from any other VP9 encoder or decoder: no implementation's code
(libvpx, libavcodec or any other) was read, and none was run in testing. The
decoder is verified against the WebM project's public VP9 test vectors, used
as data. Google offers a royalty-free patent licence for VP9 through the
WebM project, and third parties have asserted patents on VP9 as well; this
software grants no patent rights and makes no claim about whether a licence
is needed.

